{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And I have given the Levites given to Aaron, etc.</b> He doubled the word ״given״ to obligate the recipient to accept the gift, so as not to say ״it is impossible to accept this gift.״ And although in matters between one person and another one may refuse as long as the gift has not reached his hand and he has not acquired it, here He obligates them to accept this gift even though they have grounds to refuse this gift — for through their refusal the priests would be permitted to perform the Levitical service and would become liable to death for it, as our Sages of blessed memory said in the Sifrei (Numbers 18:3), and this is their language: ״גַּם הֵם גַּם אַתֶּם״ — you for yours and they for yours, in death. And although the Rambam in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Klei HaMikdash wrote, and this is his language: ״Levites who performed the service of the priests, etc., are liable to death by the hand of Heaven, but a priest who performed the service of a Levite is not liable to death but to a negative commandment,״ end quote — nevertheless there is punishment and their sacred service is diminished through the performance of sacred service. And for this reason there is grounds to refuse; therefore He doubled, saying ״given,״ against their will." "<b>From among the children of Israel.</b> The verse says ״the children of Israel״ five times in this verse without need. Our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Leviticus Rabbah 2:1) that it demonstrates affection — He doubled the mention of their names five times in one verse, corresponding to the five books of the Torah, and this is the way of derash. But according to the plain sense of the verse, it appears that the mentions of ״the children of Israel״ were stated with precision. In saying <b>from among the children of Israel</b> it refers to the entirety — as in the aforementioned statement: ״Take the Levites from among the children of Israel״ — and in saying <b>to perform the service of the children of Israel</b> it refers to the firstborn, for this service was theirs and the Levites perform their service, and therefore it was necessary to say ״the children of Israel״ and not say ״their service,״ for then the intent that the verse aimed at would have been lost. And in saying <b>and to make atonement for the children of Israel</b> it returns to the entirety, and it was careful not to say ״to make atonement for them,״ for then the intent would be that it returns to the aforementioned, namely the firstborn alone — therefore ״the children of Israel״ is stated, to say that he is speaking of the entirety. And in saying <b>and there shall be no plague among the children of Israel</b> etc., and not saying ״and there shall be no plague among you,״ it was skillful to inform that saying ״to make atonement,״ etc., and ״and there shall be no plague among you״ are not one decree dependent on the other — to make atonement, etc., so that there shall be no plague among them — but rather they are two ascending levels of goodness on this path: to make atonement for your souls from the diseases of the soul that result from sins, and furthermore, there shall be no plague among the children of Israel when they approach. And this intent is understood from what He said ״among the children of Israel,״ which indicates the separation of this statement, that it is not connected to what precedes. And in saying <b>when the children of Israel approach</b> the intent is to say that even though only the individuals approach, HaShem's wrath is kindled over the matter upon all the children of Israel. And we find a similar case, that HaShem's wrath is kindled upon the entirety because of the individual — go out and learn from the congregation of Korah, the statement of HaShem there (Numbers 16:21): ״הִבָּדְלוּ מִתּוֹךְ הָעֵדָה״ etc., and Moses needed to pray to HaShem over the matter. And see the reason there."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:02.219327070Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}